WEBVTT

1
00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:22.247
1912 the largest and most luxurious ship in the world set sail on its maiden voyage from England

2
00:00:22.307 --> 00:00:22.928
to America.

3
00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:29.193
When it left port on April 10, with over 2,200 people on board, the Titanic was believed to

4
00:00:29.233 --> 00:00:30.134
be unsinkable.

5
00:00:30.915 --> 00:00:35.506
But on April 15, the ship sank in the Atlantic Ocean after hitting an iceberg.

6
00:00:36.546 --> 00:00:44.030
Because there were not enough lifeboats for everyone, around 1,500 people—rich and poor alike—perished in the wreck.

7
00:00:44.891 --> 00:00:48.192
The story of the Titanic shocked the world when the great ship sank.

8
00:00:49.073 --> 00:00:54.036
After the sinking, many people wanted to find the ship, and some even wanted to raise it.

9
00:00:54.796 --> 00:00:58.478
But this was difficult, if not impossible, with the technology at the time.

10
00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:05.282
In the late 1970s and early 1980s, several serious expeditions tried to locate the ship.

11
00:01:06.123 --> 00:01:09.766
Using sonar, crew members scanned large areas of the seafloor.

12
00:01:10.507 --> 00:01:12.068
None of these efforts were successful.

13
00:01:13.269 --> 00:01:17.132
U.S. Navy researcher Robert Ballard was fascinated by the Titanic story.

14
00:01:17.853 --> 00:01:22.276
Even after a failed effort to locate the ship in 1977, he didn't give up.

15
00:01:23.157 --> 00:01:26.600
In the summer of 1985, Ballard worked with a French team.

16
00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.523
After scanning the seafloor with high-resolution sonar for five weeks, the search had turned up nothing.

17
00:01:33.523 --> 00:01:36.985
Ballard continued the search using a new deep-sea camera device instead.

18
00:01:37.726 --> 00:01:42.308
For nine more days, crew members saw nothing but the seafloor on the research vessel's screens.

19
00:01:42.988 --> 00:01:47.811
But just after midnight on September 1, they began to see unusual objects on the seafloor.

20
00:01:48.651 --> 00:01:51.013
Sure enough, the objects were debris from the Titanic.

21
00:01:52.093 --> 00:01:54.635
Soon researchers saw images of the shipwreck itself.

22
00:01:55.495 --> 00:01:57.036
The Titanic had been found!

23
00:01:58.008 --> 00:02:00.490
News of Ballard's discovery traveled around the world.

24
00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.792
People were eager to learn more about what happened to the ship in 1912.

25
00:02:05.712 --> 00:02:09.074
Some survivors had said that the unsinkable ship had broken apart.

26
00:02:09.815 --> 00:02:12.456
But most people assumed that the ship sank in one piece.

27
00:02:14.337 --> 00:02:18.679
This mystery was finally solved: the ship was found in two pieces.

28
00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.021
The following summer Ballard returned to the wreck.

29
00:02:22.782 --> 00:02:27.204
This time he went down 3.8 kilometers in a deep-sea submarine to see the wreck in person.

30
00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.001
The ship was rusted but well preserved.

31
00:02:30.941 --> 00:02:33.742
Some objects inside the ship remained where their owners had left them.

32
00:02:34.702 --> 00:02:39.083
Ballard explored the ship's bow and stern sections, as well as the debris field between them.

33
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:41.884
He was careful not to disturb anything.

34
00:02:42.764 --> 00:02:47.445
Since so many people had died in the disaster, he thought it would be disrespectful to take objects

35
00:02:47.505 --> 00:02:48.086
from the site.

36
00:02:50.366 --> 00:02:53.047
After Ballard's discovery many others explored the wreck.

37
00:02:54.047 --> 00:02:59.537
Not only did these scientists learn more about the ship, they also made surprising discoveries about the ocean.

38
00:03:00.337 --> 00:03:03.359
New species of bacteria were discovered in the rust on the ship.

39
00:03:04.159 --> 00:03:07.200
Scientists had expected the ocean floor to be a barren desert.

40
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.542
Instead they saw that it was home to a variety of life.

41
00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:18.125
Controversially a French expedition recovered over 1,800 objects from the wreck in 1987.

42
00:03:19.005 --> 00:03:22.546
Ballard immediately spoke out about this, believing the wreck to be a grave site.

43
00:03:23.347 --> 00:03:26.168
The remaining survivors agreed with him, but not everyone did.

44
00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.542
Future expeditions would recover even more objects.

45
00:03:30.723 --> 00:03:37.988
Many of these objects were quite ordinary: plates, forks, cups, glasses, decorations, and passengers' belongings.

46
00:03:38.808 --> 00:03:40.490
Other objects have been more surprising.

47
00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.172
Paper and clothing survived for decades on the ocean floor.

48
00:03:44.913 --> 00:03:49.756
Bottles of wine, jars of olives, and vials of perfume still seemed as good as new.

49
00:03:50.757 --> 00:03:55.440
Clocks and watches still showed times around 2:20 a.m.—the time the ship sank.

50
00:03:56.224 --> 00:03:59.507
The ship's artifacts have been displayed in exhibitions around the world.

51
00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:05.612
Supporters of recovering artifacts argue that recovering and preserving the objects helps preserve history.

52
00:04:06.592 --> 00:04:09.355
This may no longer be a debate for the Titanic's contents.

53
00:04:10.295 --> 00:04:13.878
Scientists have learned that the wreck itself is rapidly deteriorating.

54
00:04:14.759 --> 00:04:18.122
Luckily, the sad story of the Titanic is far from being lost.

55
00:04:18.982 --> 00:04:25.007
Countless books, movies, new discoveries, and exhibitions continue to tell the story of the famous ship's first, and

56
00:04:25.107 --> 00:04:25.788
only, voyage.
